MATHLIB_XPLANE_EXT(3)    Library Functions Manual    MATHLIB_XPLANE_EXT(3)

NAME
     mathlib_xplane_eval_lean, mathlib_xplane_eval_float64 – Safety and 
     floating-point evaluation extensions for the Exponent Plane

SYNOPSIS
     #include "mathlib_xplane.h"

     void
     mathlib_xplane_eval_lean(RealIntercept *node, xplane_opt_t options, ...);

     void
     mathlib_xplane_eval_float64(RealIntercept *node, xplane_opt_t options, ...);

DESCRIPTION
     The mathlib_xplane_eval_lean() and mathlib_xplane_eval_float64() functions 
     serve as hardened, specialized wrappers for the core mathlib_xplane_evaluate() 
     engine. They safeguard stack layout geometry against non-dyadic register 
     deformations, manage System V AMD64 ABI calling boundaries, and preserve 
     topological proof traces for deployment verification.

   The Lean Safety Wrapper
     The mathlib_xplane_eval_lean() function acts as a defensive gate against 
     didactic and non-dyadic register overflow shear. Complex numbers with non-
     dyadic roots (such as log(5/3) or other transfinite structures) can deform 
     the internal discrete search grid, triggering the 9th odometer overflow or 
     corrupting stack execution frames before core engine safety bounds activate.

     To eliminate high-bit register hijacking risks, this function processes 
     data loading and enforces an arbitrary top-bit protection validation gate:
           • For 1 parsed value (node->Real): The top 6 bits must be clean.
             (Mask: 0xFC00000000000000)
           • For 2 parsed values (node->Real and custom_t2): The top 3 bits of 
             each parameter must be clean. (Mask: 0xE000000000000000)

     If any prohibited top bits are active, or if the input hardware odometer 
     shear count is already degraded (initial shear >= 9), the function writes 
     the error string "xplane_eval shear too high\n" to stderr, mutates node->Real 
     to XPLANE_UNINITIALIZED (0xDEADBEEFDEADBEEF), sets node->Flags to 
     XPLANE_MASK_QUALITY_F (0x8000000000000000), and exits immediately.

     To support Lean proof extraction, this function always forces the 
     XPLANE_OPT_ACCURACY emulation bit override. It targets REX register bit 1 
     by executing a native inline assembly instruction using the r8 register. 
     This forces a verifiable system-level footprint on the stack that Lean 
     verification engines can intercept and parse cleanly.

   The Bitwise Float64 Wrapper
     The mathlib_xplane_eval_float64() function handles pure bitwise 
     quantisation from IEEE 754 64-bit floating-point variables (double) to 
     unsigned 64-bit plane integers (uint64_t).

     Natively, passing floating-point values directly to the core engine splits 
     the exact symplectic area calculation and breaks Rule 3 rotations of 
     infinity. To bypass this restriction, mathlib_xplane_eval_float64() 
     intercepts raw double parameters directly from hardware XMM registers 
     (XMM0 and XMM1, per System V AMD64 ABI convention). It copies their raw bit 
     vectors straight into node->Real and node->Imaginary using zero-overhead 
     __builtin_memcpy register moves. No destructive type-casting, fractional 
     scaling, or rounding is applied.

     The function tracks the hardware odometer gate and mirrors core engine rules 
     exactly. If the initial shear count is >= 9 and XPLANE_OPT_ACCURACY is 
     inactive, it writes "xplane_eval shear too high\n" to stderr, marks the 
     node as uninitialised, and halts. If accuracy emulation is active, it 
     bypasses the odometer failure loop and cleanly forwards execution down 
     the pipeline.

ARGUMENTS
     node       Pointer to a mutable RealIntercept structure representing the 
                active coordinate register state.

     options    Bitwise runtime configuration routing flags (xplane_opt_t).

     ...        Variadic argument stream. 
                For mathlib_xplane_eval_lean(), accepts a secondary custom tensor 
                register (custom_t2), followed by a sequential tracking override 
                parameter, terminated by XPLANE_OMIT_PARAM. If the parameter length 
                exceeds system limitations, the function triggers an immediate 
                high-shear stderr rejection.
                For mathlib_xplane_eval_float64(), accepts a literal 64-bit double 
                for the real parameter, a literal 64-bit double for the imaginary 
                parameter, and an optional trailing integer tensor register, 
                terminated by XPLANE_OMIT_PARAM.

RETURN VALUES
     Functions do not return a value. All structural transformations, register 
     rollovers, error mutations, and transfinite axis updates are performed 
     in-place directly inside the node structure.

DIAGNOSTICS
     The functions print the following message to standard error if an invalid 
     or dangerous register shear state is captured at entry:

           xplane_eval shear too high

